  SECTION 26: FILE ORGANIZATION & LIBRARIES
============================================================

  --- Header File (.h) Best Practices ---
  1. Include guards:
     #ifndef MY_HEADER_H
     #define MY_HEADER_H
     /* ... declarations ... */
     #endif /* MY_HEADER_H */

  2. Or use #pragma once (non-standard but widely supported)

  3. What belongs in headers:
     - Function prototypes
     - Type definitions (typedef, struct, enum)
     - Macro definitions
     - static inline function definitions
     - extern variable declarations

  4. What does NOT belong:
     - Function definitions (except static inline)
     - Variable definitions (use extern in header, define in .c)
     - #include of implementation-specific headers

  --- Source File (.c) Best Practices ---
  1. Include corresponding .h file first
  2. Use static for file-internal functions and variables
  3. One definition per translation unit

  --- Build Commands ---
  Compile to object:  gcc -c file.c -o file.o
  Link objects:       gcc main.o utils.o -o program
  Static library:     ar rcs libutil.a utils.o
  Link static:        gcc main.o -L. -lutil -o program
  Shared library:     gcc -shared -fPIC utils.c -o libutil.so
  Link shared:        gcc main.o -L. -lutil -o program
  Run with shared:    LD_LIBRARY_PATH=. ./program

  --- Opaque Pointer Pattern (Encapsulation) ---
  /* In header: */
  typedef struct Vector Vector;  /* forward declaration */
  Vector *vec_create(void);
  void vec_push(Vector *v, int x);
  void vec_destroy(Vector *v);

  /* In source: */
  struct Vector { int *data; size_t len, cap; };
  /* Users can't access internals ΓÇö true encapsulation */

